{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004862","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 8 candidates are direct matches to dilated cardiomyopathy by trait_efo, with generally coherent phenotype fields (either generic DCM, non-ischemic DCM, or clinical DCM). Per the provided domain guidance, phenotype alignment is primary and MTAG is not a negative; within the same study family, the MTAG-enhanced sibling is mildly preferred when endpoints/covariates are comparable.

PGS004862 (Dilated cardiomyopathy (MTAG), Nat Genet 2024-04-18) is preferred over its non-MTAG sibling PGS004861 because it has slightly stronger reported full-model discrimination (AUROC 0.71 vs 0.70; R² 0.05 vs 0.048) and a stronger per-SD effect size (OR 1.76 vs 1.56) with the same basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10) and large European validation (n=347,585). Compared with the later 2024-12-16 family (PGS004946–PGS004951), those models have heterogeneous phenotype definitions (non-ischemic vs clinical DCM) and evaluation ancestry strings that include 'Not reported' for several, and their reported R² values appear to be liability-scale/full-model variants rather than clearly PRS-only metrics; thus they are harder to compare cleanly across candidates.

Limitation: none of the candidates provides an explicitly PRS-only AUROC/R² in the top-level fields, and most reported AUROCs are full-model values with covariates, so cross-model performance comparability is limited; this keeps the direct-match choice from being high-confidence/high-quality based on the visible evidence alone."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004911",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by trait_efo, but PGS004910 and PGS004911 provide the cleanest endpoint alignment (phenotyping_reported: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy), modern genome-wide method (PRS-CS), and large European evaluation (n=343,182; EVAL: EUR 100%). Between these two near-clones from the same publication with identical covariates (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10) and similar variant counts, PGS004911 is mildly preferred because it is the MTAG-enhanced version (domain guidance: MTAG is a legitimate power boost and mildly preferred within the same study family) and it shows stronger reported full-model discrimination and variance explained (AUROC 0.8; R² 0.048 vs AUROC 0.73; R² 0.031) plus slightly stronger effect sizes (HR 2.34; OR 1.33). Main limitation: the reported AUROC/R² appear to be full-model metrics (not explicitly PRS-only), so cross-model comparability is imperfect and confidence is not high; additionally, PGS000739 reports a higher full-model AUROC (0.821) but with much heavier clinical covariates, making that AUROC less informative for standalone PRS quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000114",
  "confidence": "High",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are from the same disease-focused publication family (Ann Rheum Dis; SparSNP; basic covariates sex + PCs; EUR dev/eval), so the primary differentiator is phenotype alignment to the target trait (juvenile idiopathic arthritis). PGS000114 is the only model whose trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported are the broad JIA endpoint rather than a specific JIA subtype (enthesitis-related, oligoarticular, RF-negative polyarthritis), making it the most faithful direct match for a generic JIA target. Its reported validation evidence is coherent (full-model AUROC 0.738 with CI; OR per SD 2.085), and there is no indication of heavy clinical covariate packaging. Main limitation: the available AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (not explicitly PGS-only), which reduces comparability of performance across candidates; however, endpoint fidelity dominates here and supports selecting the broad JIA model."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000077",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for the target concept (lymphoid/lymphocytic leukemia) are present (e.g., PGS000077, PGS000645, PGS000788, PGS002305; plus several chronic lymphocytic leukemia models which are subtype-aligned but not exact). PGS000077 is the most coherent direct-match choice on endpoint fidelity and comparability: trait_efo matches lymphoid leukemia and phenotyping_reported is a direct incident lymphocytic leukemia endpoint with only basic covariates (age/sex/array/PCs), and it reports supportive effect size evidence (HR=1.45 per SD) alongside large European evaluation (n=391,338). However, confidence is limited because the available discrimination metrics are full-model (AUROC/C-index) rather than explicitly PRS-only, and a closely related near-clone (PGS000788) reports higher full-model AUROC/HR but is explicitly derived from PGS000077 with inverse weights, creating unresolved conflict about which is the better standalone PRS from the visible metadata alone."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (sleep apnea), including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several clinically dominant subtype models for obstructive sleep apnea (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge indicates obstructive sleep apnea is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable direct matches and can be preferred when better supported.

Among the OSA candidates, PGS005220 is best supported by the visible evidence: it targets obstructive sleep apnea, uses a multi-score aggregation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”), is developed in a multi-cohort setting (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP), and reports strong effect size evidence (OR=1.53 per SD, 95% CI 1.39–1.68) alongside a reported full-model AUROC of 0.79. Compared with the ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” framework cluster (mostly full-model AUROC ~0.49–0.53 with small effect sizes near null), PGS005220 shows materially stronger discriminative signal.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (and covariates include BMI), with no explicit PGS-only AUROC/R2 provided, so the PRS-only contribution cannot be cleanly compared across candidates; additionally, there is competition from closely related OSA models (e.g., PGS005219 with similar AUROC and a larger OR but BMI-adjusted phenotype). These comparability gaps prevent a high-confidence ‘best’ designation."}

